<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Applies to every `dotnet test` in this repository, wired in by Directory.Build.props.

  TestSessionTimeout is here because a deadlocked test used to hang the suite
  indefinitely rather than fail it: nothing in dotnet test, the CI job or the local
  gate bounds a test run, so a stuck reader waits for as long as anybody lets it.

  Four hours, because the 2026-10-04 isolated Mod session completed 1008 tests in
  153m25s with a ten-minute blame-hang bound and no hung test or dump. Its slowest
  test took 146.56s. The uncapped full-suite Arbiter session completed in 5m09s,
  while Mod exhausted both the 45-minute and two-hour session bounds.
  Four hours leaves 86m35s (56 percent) above the measured complete Mod session;
  the bound applies per test assembly, not to the sum of serialized assemblies.
  This is headroom for healthy rows, not permission to reinterpret an abort.

  Keep the bound even when a suite grows. Raise it rather than removing it, and
  investigate individual tests with blame-hang diagnostics before calling them hung.
  Test assemblies share no engine state and run one at a time in test-session.sh;
  their collection budget defaults to this M4 Max's twelve performance cores.
  RUNMOBILE_TEST_THREADS overrides that budget in the default scripted session.

  What an abort is worth is a separate question, and ./scripts/test-session.sh owns
  it: `dotnet test` reports an aborted run as "Passed!" with a partial total, so the
  verdict for a session comes from that script and never from the printed count.
-->
<RunSettings>
  <RunConfiguration>
    <TestSessionTimeout>14400000</TestSessionTimeout>
    <MaxCpuCount>1</MaxCpuCount>
  </RunConfiguration>
  <xUnit>
    <MaxParallelThreads>12</MaxParallelThreads>
  </xUnit>
</RunSettings>
